It's worth noting that in slashem, polymorphed items (and monster)
eventually revert back to their original form. I know some people here
disregard any and all slashem features out of hand but I think this is
a good one. The thought of having your silver daggers turn back into a
pile of darts in the middle gehemonoemenon is quite deliciously evil.
Also, it makes all polymorph behaviour consistent and goes some way to
balancing polypiling. The balance isn't perfect, it could do with a
little tweaking, but its' definitely a step in the right direction.

Obviously one counter to poly-reversion is to just use the polypiled
item immediately (scrolls/ potions/ markers/ eating rings) before it
gets a chance to revert. This is fine as far as I'm concerned, because
it's certainly no more powerful than vanilla polymorph.

The other counter is to "fix" the item by dipping it in a potion of
Restore Ability. This is where I think slashem's poly-reversion needs
tweaking. Quite apart from the fact that *restore* ability should,
logically, revert the item back to its original form, IMO this is the
wrong choice of potion for the job. RA is hardly used otherwise, and
it's common enough (thanks to polypiling, ironically, as well as
slashem's gem alchemy) that you will have enough potions to fix pretty
much everything that needs it.

You could remove the "fix" option altogether, but I would suggest
simply changing the method of fixation: Instead of dipping the item(s)
to be fixed, stack it/them with a ring of sustain ability/ amulet of
unchanging and zap polymorph again. In this case the items would be
fixed instead of polymorphed, the ring/ amulet would be consumed, and
your items would face the usual risk of destruction through shuddering
according to stack size. This would leave polypiling with a
significant drawback (polypiled items are only temporary) that can be
overcome with some effort/ expenditure of sparse resources (ring/
amulet) and the risk of losing your new item(s). Polypile reversion
also has the bonus of being coded already=3D-)

However, even with fixing as it is, polypiling in slashem is still
less productive and more hassle than in vanilla.


